.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt WEATHER 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm weather
.Nd print current weather conditions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 1dhqsSV
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl l Ar lat,lon
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl p Ar sources
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Fl u Ar metric|imperial
.Op Fl v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
fetches the current conditions for a place over an encrypted (TLS) connection and prints them.
It needs no proxy and no other software; the TLS library and the trust anchors it needs are built in.
.Pp
The place, units and other settings come from
.Xr weatherrc 5 ,
and the options below override them for one run.
Diagnostics go to standard error (or the system log with
.Fl s ) ;
standard output carries only the result, so the output is safe to use in scripts.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 1
Print a single line instead of the multi-line summary.
.It Fl c Ar file
Read settings from
.Ar file
instead of
.Pa ~/.weatherrc .
The file must exist, unless
.Fl S
is given, which creates it.
.It Fl d
Debug: log at the most detailed level and include the request and response text.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl H Ar host:port
Testing only: speak plain HTTP to
.Ar host:port
instead of TLS to the data source.
A warning is logged because nothing is encrypted or verified.
.It Fl l Ar lat,lon
Latitude and longitude in decimal degrees, for example
.Ql 43.0747,-89.3841 .
This also forgets the configured place name, since it described somewhere else; use
.Fl n
to name the new place.
.It Fl n Ar name
Name of the place, shown in the full output (printable ASCII, no
.Ql # ) .
.It Fl p Ar sources
Comma separated list of data sources, tried in order until one answers.
The sources are
.Ql open-meteo
(the default) and
.Ql met-no
(MET Norway, worldwide).
.Ql met-no
needs a
.Ic contact
in the settings file, because its terms of use require clients to identify themselves; without one it is skipped.
.It Fl q
Log errors only.
.It Fl s
Log to
.Xr syslog 3
instead of standard error.
.It Fl S
Save the location, name and units (after applying any other options) into the settings file and exit.
Comments and all other settings in the file are kept; the file is replaced atomically.
.It Fl t Ar seconds
Network timeout for each connect, read and write. Once a connection is made, one attempt as a whole (handshake and answer) is given up on after about three times this.
.It Fl u Ar units
Either
.Ql metric
(degrees Celsius, km/h) or
.Ql imperial
(degrees Fahrenheit, mph).
.It Fl v
Log more.
Give it twice for the most detail.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Pp
Transient failures (name lookup, connection, timeout, dropped connection, server errors) are retried
with a doubling delay, up to the number of
.Va attempts
in the settings file.
Permanent failures (such as a certificate that cannot be trusted) are not retried.
If several sources are given, a failing source is skipped and the next one is tried.
.Pp
.Nm
checks the server certificate against the built-in trust anchors and against the system clock.
If the clock is earlier than 2025 it refuses to continue and says so, because certificates cannot be checked.
Interrupting
.Nm
with
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM
during a fetch cancels it cleanly.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.weatherrc"
.It Pa ~/.weatherrc
Settings, see
.Xr weatherrc 5 .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "WEATHER_NO_TA"
.It Ev HOME
Where to look for the settings file.
.It Ev WEATHER_NOW
Testing only: a Unix time in seconds that replaces the system clock.
.It Ev WEATHER_NO_TA
Testing only: when set, no trust anchors are loaded, so TLS verification must fail.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success and otherwise with a value from
.In sysexits.h
that says what went wrong:
.Bl -column "130" "EX_UNAVAILABLE" -offset indent
.It 64 Ta EX_USAGE Ta bad command line
.It 65 Ta EX_DATAERR Ta unusable or malformed answer
.It 68 Ta EX_NOHOST Ta name lookup failed
.It 69 Ta EX_UNAVAILABLE Ta cannot connect, or every source failed
.It 71 Ta EX_OSERR Ta the system clock is wrong
.It 74 Ta EX_IOERR Ta network I/O error, or a truncated answer
.It 75 Ta EX_TEMPFAIL Ta timed out
.It 76 Ta EX_PROTOCOL Ta HTTP error, TLS failure or untrusted certificate
.It 78 Ta EX_CONFIG Ta bad settings file
.It 130 Ta Ta interrupted
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show the weather for the configured place:
.Pp
.Dl $ weather
.Pp
One line in metric units for Oslo, for a status bar:
.Pp
.Dl $ weather -1 -u metric -l 59.91,10.75
.Pp
Make Madison the default place:
.Pp
.Dl $ weather -l 43.0747,-89.3841 -n \&"Madison, WI\&" -S
.Pp
Use it in a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if line=$(weather -1 -q); then echo "$line"; else echo "weather unavailable"; fi
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xweather 1 ,
.Xr weatherrc 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Jack Willis
.Sh BUGS
Only TLS 1.2 is supported by the built-in TLS library.
The trust anchors are compiled in (ISRG Root X1 and X2, and HARICA TLS RSA Root CA 2021), so a data source whose certificates chain to another
root cannot be used until the program is rebuilt with that root.
Name lookups cannot be interrupted, so cancelling during one takes effect when the lookup ends.
